<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<phpunit xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="vendor/phpunit/phpunit/phpunit.xsd"
         bootstrap="vendor/autoload.php"
         colors="true"
>
    <testsuites>
        <testsuite name="Unit">
            <directory>tests/Unit</directory>
        </testsuite>
        <testsuite name="Feature">
            <directory>tests/Feature</directory>
        </testsuite>
    </testsuites>
    <source>
        <include>
            <directory>app</directory>
        </include>
    </source>
    <php>
        <env name="APP_ENV" value="testing"/>
        <env name="APP_MAINTENANCE_DRIVER" value="file"/>
        <env name="BCRYPT_ROUNDS" value="4"/>
        <env name="BROADCAST_CONNECTION" value="null"/>
        <env name="CACHE_STORE" value="array"/>

        <!--
            Tests run against a REAL PostgreSQL database, not sqlite.
            The schema depends on jsonb, GIN indexes, tsvector, pg_trgm and
            jsonb_exists() - none of which sqlite has - so an in-memory sqlite
            run would fail at the first migration and prove nothing.

            Create it once:
              psql -U postgres -c "CREATE DATABASE remarket_test;"

            Host, user and password are inherited from .env; only the database
            name is overridden, so tests can never touch your dev data.
        -->
        <env name="DB_CONNECTION" value="pgsql"/>
        <env name="DB_DATABASE" value="remarket_test"/>

        <!--
            Turnstile OFF for the suite, pinned here rather than inherited.

            THIS IS NOT A CONVENIENCE, IT IS A BUG FIX. Until 20 Sep these two
            were not set at all, so whether the suite passed depended on what
            happened to be in the developer's .env. The keys were blank, so
            Turnstile disabled itself, so verify() returned true, so every test
            that submits a form passed — and the whole suite quietly rested on
            the bot defence being switched off.

            The moment the keys were filled in — which is a thing that MUST
            happen before launch — sixteen tests failed at once, across auth,
            password reset, offers and reports. Not one of them was about
            Turnstile, and the failure message („Проверката за робот не мина")
            pointed at the forms rather than at the configuration.

            A test environment that changes behaviour because of a production
            setting is not a test environment. Pinned blank, on purpose: no
            test should have to carry a token to exercise the thing it is
            actually about.

            Turnstile's OWN behaviour is covered by TurnstileTest, which turns
            the keys on per-test and fakes the HTTP call. That is where it
            belongs — not as an invisible precondition of every other test on
            the site.
        -->
        <env name="TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY" value=""/>
        <env name="TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY" value=""/>

        <!--
            Billing, pinned blank for the same reason as Turnstile above.

            With no IBAN and no VAT ground, BillingIdentity says „not ready" and
            the top-up flow is closed. That is the state every test that is not
            about billing should see — and pinning it means the suite cannot
            start depending on whatever happens to be in a developer's .env.
            Precisely the trap Turnstile fell into: the keys were not pinned,
            so sixteen unrelated tests quietly relied on the bot defence being
            switched off.

            PaymentTest turns these on per-test, which is where they belong.
        -->
        <!--
            New-account moderation, pinned OFF — and pinned is the point.

            This was never pinned, so the whole suite quietly inherited
            whatever a developer had in their .env. That is the third time this
            has bitten (Turnstile, then billing): a test that depends on an
            unpinned env value passes or fails according to a file that is not
            in the repository, and the failure looks like the feature is broken.

            ZERO rather than two, because zero is what the suite has been
            running with and pinning it to two would flip a precondition under
            hundreds of tests at once. The baseline is „the hold is off"; every
            test that exercises the hold turns it on for itself with
            config(['remarket.antispam.moderated_listings_for_new_accounts' => 2])
            — WantedTest and BulkImportTest both do.
        -->
        <env name="NEW_ACCOUNT_MODERATED_LISTINGS" value="0"/>

        <env name="BILLING_IBAN" value=""/>
        <env name="BILLING_BIC" value=""/>
        <env name="BILLING_BANK" value=""/>
        <env name="BILLING_VAT_REGISTERED" value="false"/>
        <env name="BILLING_VAT_NUMBER" value=""/>
        <env name="BILLING_VAT_EXEMPT_NOTE" value=""/>

        <env name="MAIL_MAILER" value="array"/>
        <env name="QUEUE_CONNECTION" value="sync"/>
        <env name="SESSION_DRIVER" value="array"/>
        <env name="PULSE_ENABLED" value="false"/>
        <env name="TELESCOPE_ENABLED" value="false"/>
        <env name="NIGHTWATCH_ENABLED" value="false"/>
    </php>
</phpunit>
